{0 Performance}

How fast Silica is, how it is measured, where it is slower than other
frameworks and why, and the budgets that the tests enforce.

{1:machine Machine and method}

All numbers here come from one machine: 512 CPUs (AMD EPYC 9755), Linux,
headless Chromium 156, Node 22. The machine is shared with other builds, so a
number moves by about 10 percent from one run to the next. Compare numbers of
the same run only. The benchmarks are in [bench/]:

- [bench/browser/run.ts] measures in Chromium through Playwright. A time is from
  just before the click (or the input event) to the end of the next frame, with
  vsync off, so it includes style, layout and paint. Each measured run uses a
  fresh page, after untimed warm-up rounds; the tables give the median. CPU
  throttling (4x) is set with the DevTools protocol. Memory is the JavaScript
  heap after a garbage collection.
- [bench/keyed] is the keyed rows app of js-framework-benchmark written with
  Silica; [bench/compare] has the same app in plain DOM JavaScript (the floor),
  React 19 and Solid 1.9 (compiled JSX). All are minified by esbuild.
- [bench/server/bench.exe] renders pages in process, through the same entry as
  the server ([handle], session, settle, render). [bench/server/wrk.sh] runs
  [wrk -t4 -c32] against the real servers. [bench/server/live_fanout.ts]
  connects many Live sessions to one topic.

{1:size Bundle size}

The production build (see {!page-deploy.build}), in bytes after brotli 11:

{table
  {tr {th Bundle} {th Brotli} {th Raw}}
  {tr {td Site, first load: entry and shared chunk} {td 67 910} {td 286 435}}
  {tr {td Site, lazy chunk of the component pages} {td 19 383} {td 113 346}}
  {tr {td Site, CSS} {td 5 663} {td 29 821}}
  {tr {td Mini Linear, JavaScript} {td 65 347} {td 296 301}}
  {tr {td Keyed rows app: Silica} {td 41 648} {td 166 205}}
  {tr {td Keyed rows app: React 19} {td 60 500} {td 226 800}}
  {tr {td Keyed rows app: Solid} {td 6 350} {td 17 200}}
  {tr {td Keyed rows app: plain JavaScript} {td 1 130} {td 2 760}}
}

The Silica rows come from [dune test]; the three other keyed rows apps come
from the browser driver, which reports 0.1 KiB steps, so their bytes are
rounded.

Before this work the site was one file of 1 333 609 bytes, 125 336 brotli.
The smallest Silica app is about 41 kB brotli: the runtime, the renderer, the
JSON codecs and the OCaml standard library parts that Melange links. Of that,
the [Printf] machinery is about 30 kB raw: [Printexc] pulls it in.

{1:keyed Keyed rows}

Milliseconds, median, final tree, CPU 1x. Silica and the plain JavaScript
version also ran back to back on the v0.12 tree ("Silica before").

{table
  {tr {th Operation} {th Silica before} {th Silica} {th Plain JS} {th React 19} {th Solid}}
  {tr {td Create 1,000 rows} {td 45.9} {td 46.8} {td 46.1} {td 41.8} {td 47.4}}
  {tr {td Replace all 1,000 rows} {td 58.1} {td 49.7} {td 49.7} {td 55.2} {td 54.9}}
  {tr {td Update every 10th row} {td 27.3} {td 18.5} {td 13.6} {td 13.6} {td 14.2}}
  {tr {td Select a row} {td 13.2} {td 5.6} {td 0.4} {td 0.9} {td 0.5}}
  {tr {td Swap two rows} {td 53.8} {td 13.5} {td 5.7} {td 41.9} {td 6.2}}
  {tr {td Remove a row} {td 22.8} {td 15.2} {td 9.0} {td 8.3} {td 9.3}}
  {tr {td Create 10,000 rows} {td failed} {td 635.5} {td 418.1} {td 533.8} {td 401.1}}
  {tr {td Append 1,000 rows} {td 66.3} {td 83.5} {td 60.3} {td 52.1} {td 55.0}}
  {tr {td Clear 1,000 rows} {td 4.9} {td 4.0} {td 4.3} {td 7.1} {td 5.1}}
}

With the CPU 4x slower: select 56.1 ms before and 33.5 ms now (plain
JavaScript 4.3), swap 205.8 and 55.2 (plain 26.6, React 178.1), create 10,000
rows failed before and takes 2645 ms now (plain 1634, Solid 1733).

Heap after create 1,000: Silica 4.88 MB, plain 2.13, React 4.48, Solid 2.95.

A keystroke in a filtered list of 1,000 rows takes 2.9 ms (15.6 ms at 4x);
clearing the filter, which adds back 1,000 rows, takes 9.6 ms (47.9 ms at 4x).

{2:slower Where Silica is slower, and why}

- Select, update, remove: Silica runs the view of the component that changed
  and compares the whole result with the last one. Plain JavaScript and Solid
  change only the nodes that depend on the change. So the time of a small
  change grows with the size of the view of that component: about 5 ms for
  1,000 rows. Split a large view into components: a component whose snapshot
  and props did not change is not rendered again (the render skip).
- Create 10,000 rows is about 50 percent slower than plain JavaScript and
  about 20 percent slower than React: each row is built three times (the view,
  the resolved view of the instance tree, the virtual DOM nodes).
- Memory: each virtual node keeps its DOM node and each instance keeps its last
  view, so a page holds about twice the heap of plain JavaScript.
- Bundle: the smallest app is about 7 times the size of Solid's (see above).

What changed in v0.13: each virtual node keeps its DOM node, attributes are
compared with the last view and not read from the DOM, keyed children use a
key table and move only the nodes outside the longest increasing run, and
children are arrays (the list functions of Melange overflowed the stack at
10,000 rows).

{1:loads Page loads}

Server render plus hydration, median ms: first contentful paint, then the
time the root gets [data-silica-ready]. The first number of each pair is the
v0.12 tree, run back to back.

{table
  {tr {th Page} {th CPU} {th Before} {th Now}}
  {tr {td Site [/]} {td 1x} {td 60 / 35.1} {td 52 / 33.7}}
  {tr {td Site [/docs/router]} {td 1x} {td 80 / 46.6} {td 60 / 39.5}}
  {tr {td Site [/components/select], a lazy route} {td 1x} {td } {td 40 / 25.9}}
  {tr {td Mini Linear, 1000 issues} {td 1x} {td 204 / 144.4} {td 152 / 115.3}}
  {tr {td Site [/]} {td 4x} {td 212 / 155.0} {td 104 / 141.3}}
  {tr {td Site [/docs/router]} {td 4x} {td 236 / 162.4} {td 148 / 229.8}}
  {tr {td Site [/components/select], a lazy route} {td 4x} {td } {td 120 / 168.5}}
  {tr {td Mini Linear, 1000 issues} {td 4x} {td 220 / 626.1} {td 224 / 657.1}}
}

At 4x the docs page is ready later than before (229.8 ms against 162.4): the
entry now imports a shared chunk, one more file to fetch and run. The first
paint is earlier on every site page. The mini Linear list with 1000 issues
spends its time in hydration: the page is 784 kB of HTML and the answers of
the settle step hold every issue twice (in the view and in the answer table).

{1:server Server}

In process, median ms per page (v0.12 in parentheses): site landing 0.191
(0.226), largest docs page 0.747 (0.925), mini Linear with 1000 issues 7.85
(13.4).

Requests per second, [wrk -t4 -c32 -d10s], v0.12 and the final build run back
to back:

{table
  {tr {th Target} {th Before} {th Now}}
  {tr {td Site landing} {td 3 547} {td 5 553}}
  {tr {td Largest docs page} {td 757} {td 1 250}}
  {tr {td Mini Linear, 1000 issues} {td 60} {td 112}}
  {tr {td The entry script, brotli accepted} {td 565 (1.3 MB, not compressed)} {td 18 731}}
  {tr {td Health} {td 90 245} {td 134 557}}
}

One OCaml domain renders, so pages per second is about one over the render
time. HTML is not compressed by the server: a proxy does it (see
{!page-deploy.build}).

{1:live Live}

From the press of "Create issue" in one mini Linear page to the new row in
another page (the call, the handler, the publish, the WebSocket message, the
step and the paint): 3 ms with 30 issues, 19 ms with 1000 (17 and 68.5 ms at
4x).

1000 sessions on one topic, from the create call to the state at the last
socket: 11 ms with 30 issues; 156 ms with 1000 issues, when each message is
about 250 kB. The server holds 122 MB with 1000 sessions and 30 issues, 889 MB
with 1000 issues. A topic sends its full state to every subscriber, so the
cost grows with the state size times the subscribers. Before v0.13 the adapter
accepted at most 64 connections, so 1000 sessions could not connect.

{1:budgets Budgets}

- Size: [dune test --profile release] fails when a production bundle grows
  over its brotli budget ([test/budget/budgets.json]: the site first load, its
  lazy chunks and CSS, mini Linear, the keyed rows app). The budgets are the
  sizes above plus about 2 percent. A development build carries the hot reload
  code of [silica dev], so the check runs on the release profile only; a debug
  build ([SILICA_MINIFY=false]) skips it.
- Time: [dune build @bench --force] runs the server benchmarks, [wrk] and the
  browser benchmarks of Silica and plain JavaScript, and fails when a number
  is over its budget in [bench/budgets.json]. The time budgets are about three
  times the medians above (four times for a single keystroke), and the
  requests per second at least a third of them, because the machine is shared.
  It writes [bench/results/latest.md]. It needs Chromium at [/usr/bin/chromium]
  (or [CHROME]) and the comparison apps built ([npm run build] in
  [bench/compare]).
